(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-pulm-bronchitis-cap-quiz"]={"title":"Acute Bronchitis & Community-Acquired Pneumonia Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"An outpatient with community-acquired pneumonia has risk factors for resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae and a beta-lactam allergy. Which drug fits?","choices":["Cefpodoxime","Amoxicillin","Azithromycin","Moxifloxacin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A respiratory fluoroquinolone (levofloxacin or moxifloxacin) is the alternative to beta-lactam plus macrolide and is good for patients with a beta-lactam allergy. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which exposure is a listed risk factor for penicillin-resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae?","choices":["Living in a nursing home","Attending hemodialysis","Living in a college dormitory","A child who attends day care"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Exposure to a child in a day care center is listed with age over 65, a beta-lactam within 3 months, alcoholism, immunosuppression and medical comorbidities. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A patient with acute bronchitis has documented Mycoplasma infection. Which antibiotic is preferred?","choices":["Azithromycin","Erythromycin","Doxycycline","Levofloxacin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Doxycycline is preferred when Mycoplasma is documented, because of macrolide resistance. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"An outpatient with community-acquired pneumonia has no risk factors for resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae. Which drug is first line?","choices":["Doxycycline","Azithromycin","Levofloxacin","Cefpodoxime"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With no risk of resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae, first line is a macrolide (azithromycin or clarithromycin); doxycycline and minocycline are second line. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which group of pathogens is the common cause of acute bronchitis?","choices":["Atypical bacteria","Gram-positive cocci","Respiratory viruses","Gram-negative bacilli"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The common causes are viruses (rhinovirus, coronavirus, influenza, adenovirus); bacteria are only occasional causes, sometimes secondary to a viral illness. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Trauma is the example given for which route by which microbes reach the lung?","choices":["Oropharyngeal aspiration","Direct inoculation","Aerosol inhalation","Hematogenous spread"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Direct inoculation is the route of entry given with trauma as its example, alongside aspiration, inhalation and hematogenous spread. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which beta-lactam is listed for intravenous use in hospitalized patients with community-acquired pneumonia outside the intensive care unit?","choices":["Cefpodoxime","Cefotaxime","Cefepime","Ceftazidime"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The intravenous beta-lactams for ward patients are cefotaxime, ceftriaxone and high-dose ampicillin, each given with a macrolide or doxycycline. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Alongside an intravenous beta-lactam for a hospitalized ward patient with pneumonia, which drug may stand in for the macrolide?","choices":["Vancomycin","Gentamicin","Doxycycline","Linezolid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The ward regimen is an intravenous beta-lactam plus an intravenous or oral macrolide, or doxycycline in place of the macrolide. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"An outpatient with community-acquired pneumonia and no resistance risk factors needs a second-line option. Which drug is listed?","choices":["Moxifloxacin","Cefuroxime","Ceftriaxone","Minocycline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Without risk of resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae, first line is a macrolide and second line is doxycycline or minocycline. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How is community-acquired pneumonia usually managed in the outpatient setting?","choices":["Empiric antibiotics, no routine tests","Blood cultures before antibiotics","Sputum culture to choose the drug","Sputum gram stain before treating"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Outpatients do well with empiric antibiotics, and diagnostic tests are not routinely performed. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Why may antihistamines be avoided in acute bronchitis?","choices":["They increase mucus production","They suppress the cough reflex","They dry out bronchial secretions","They promote antibiotic resistance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antihistamines and sympathomimetics may be avoided because they dehydrate bronchial secretions and may prolong symptoms. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which route by which microbes reach the lung is common in elderly patients?","choices":["Aspiration from the oropharynx","Inhalation of infectious aerosols","Hematogenous dissemination","Direct inoculation by trauma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Microbes enter the lung by aspiration from the oropharynx (common in the elderly), inhalation of aerosols, hematogenous spread or direct inoculation. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"In acute bronchitis, a respiratory fluoroquinolone is avoided unless which history is present?","choices":["Recent antibiotic exposure","Documented Mycoplasma infection","A viral cause such as influenza","Fever along with the cough"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In acute bronchitis a respiratory fluoroquinolone is to be avoided unless the patient has had recent antibiotic exposure. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 8"}]};
